//? Title: K-medoids through actual candidate swaps
//? Subtitle: Random starts, Euclidean objectives and the original contaminated three-blob example
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Clustering by medoids chooses actual input points to represent groups while
minimizing a sum of distances. Selecting the globally best k representatives is
combinatorial; this implementation uses a seeded random start and complete
one-swap neighborhoods. We inspect actual proposed and committed objectives,
retain the original three noisy blobs and two outliers, and distinguish an
exhausted neighborhood from an iteration limit. Input-point representatives do
not make the fitted clustering invariant to contamination.
)

\toc

> The objective and the actual variant

For finite points x_i in a common positive dimension and k distinct
representative identities M, minimize

$$ J(M)=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\min_{m\in M}\lVert x_i-x_m\rVert_2. $$
\alttext(The objective sums each point’s Euclidean distance to its closest selected input representative.)

The library precomputes a symmetric distance matrix. A seeded mt19937_64 shuffle
chooses k distinct identities. This is random initialization followed by
PAM-style SWAP, not Kaufman–Rousseeuw’s greedy BUILD and not FastPAM. Every scan
visits medoid slots then original non-medoids. A proposal replaces the running
scan incumbent only when its rounded cost is smaller by more than the absolute
threshold 1e-12. The last such proposal is committed, its assignment recomputed
once, and the process repeats. Distance ties use the first medoid slot; distinct
identities at coincident coordinates can yield empty clusters.

A no-improvement exit means no one-swap proposal beats the current objective by
that threshold. It does not establish the global optimum. A limit exit has not
tested the final neighborhood. Zero iterations returns only initialization.
Actual history stores the evaluated proposals separately from accepted
trajectory states, so a plot of every proposal need not be monotone, while
committed costs strictly decrease.

> Three points: see proposals before commitment

On the line at0,2,10 with k=1, direct sums are12,10,18. The middle point is the
unique optimum. The diagram uses the actual initial identity, first evaluated
proposal and final result; it does not infer an unobserved path.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_medoids_proposals.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/k_medoids.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::k_medoids_trace({{0}, {2}, {10}}, 1, 100, 0);
    std::print("| actual scan | slot | proposed point | objective | new scan best |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.candidates)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} | {} |\n", e.iteration, e.slot,
                   e.point, e.cost, e.best_changed);
    std::print("\nFinal medoid {}; objective {:.12f}; accepted swaps {}; exhausted neighborhood {}.\n",
               t.result.medoids[0], t.result.total_cost, t.result.iterations,
               t.stop == dm::algorithms::KMedoidsStop::NoImprovement);
    const auto &e = t.candidates.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("start", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual initial medoid {}\ncost {}",
                       t.states.front().medoids[0], t.states.front().cost)).node("trial", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual first proposal {}\ncost {}", e.point, e.cost)).node("finish", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual final medoid {}\ncost {}", t.result.medoids[0], t.result.total_cost)).edge("start", "trial").edge("trial", "finish").save_svg("build/k_medoids_proposals.svg");
}
```
