> A complete limit is different from convergence

The fixed line0,1,2,8,9,10 has a two-medoid global optimum of4 at identities1
and4. Every fit below is a separate complete experiment with its actual
iteration limit, not a reconstruction of one fit from repeated prefixes. The
trace’s termination reason distinguishes a tested local optimum from a limit.
Full-budget seeds need not find the same global optimum on arbitrary data.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_medoids_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/k_medoids.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>> data = {{0}, {1}, {2}, {8}, {9}, {10}};
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| actual limit | accepted swaps | actual cost | exhausted neighborhood |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t limit : {0, 1, 2, 5, 20}) {
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::k_medoids_trace(data, 2, limit, 7);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {:.12f} | {} |\n", limit, t.result.iterations,
                   t.result.total_cost,
                   t.stop == dm::algorithms::KMedoidsStop::NoImprovement);
        x.push_back(limit);
        y.push_back(t.result.total_cost);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "fit", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).line({0, 20}, {4, 4}, "exact best", {220, 38, 38}, 2).y_limits(0, 20).x_label("actual scan ceiling").y_label("distance sum").title("Complete fits versus a small exact optimum").save_svg("build/k_medoids_limits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual limit | accepted swaps | actual cost | exhausted neighborhood |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 0 | 0 | 4.000000000000 | false |
| 1 | 0 | 4.000000000000 | true |
| 2 | 0 | 4.000000000000 | true |
| 5 | 0 | 4.000000000000 | true |
| 20 | 0 | 4.000000000000 | true |
\image(figures/k_medoids_limits.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Separate complete capped fits on the same small exact control.)
\alttext(Computed objectives are compared with the independently enumerable two-medoid optimum of4; the table records whether the final neighborhood was tested.)

> Checking actual selected work

For n points and k medoids, each complete swap scan evaluates exactly k(n−k)
proposals, and each proposal computes n*k distance lookups. There is one initial
assignment pass and one additional pass per accepted swap. Thus the recorded
lookup count equals assignment_passes*n*k; the matrix evaluates n(n−1)/2
unordered distances. These are producing counters, excluding validation,
coordinate norm work, shuffling, allocations, copies and elapsed time. Matrix
preparation costs O(n²d) and a naive full scan O(k²n(n−k)); this is deliberately
not the faster cached nearest/second-nearest PAM implementation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/k_medoids_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/k_medoids.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| n | candidate evaluations | assignment passes | actual lookups | passes times n times k | " "distance pairs |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {8, 16, 32, 64, 128}) {
        std::vector<std::vector<double>> data;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            data.push_back({double(i), double(i % 3)});
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::k_medoids_trace(data, 2, 1, 7);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, t.work.candidates,
                   t.work.assignment_passes, t.work.distance_lookups,
                   t.work.assignment_passes * n * 2, t.work.distance_pairs);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        y.push_back(std::log2(t.work.distance_lookups));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("One complete best-swap scan: actual lookups").x_label("log2(n)").y_label("log2(actual distance lookups)").save_svg("build/k_medoids_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | candidate evaluations | assignment passes | actual lookups | passes times n times k | distance pairs |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 12 | 14 | 224 | 224 | 28 |
| 16 | 28 | 30 | 960 | 960 | 120 |
| 32 | 60 | 62 | 3968 | 3968 | 496 |
| 64 | 124 | 126 | 16128 | 16128 | 2016 |
| 128 | 252 | 254 | 65024 | 65024 | 8128 |
\image(figures/k_medoids_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual lookup counts for one full scan, including any committed assignment.)
\alttext(The native count is checked against assignment passes times n times k; it is not a timing or total arithmetic benchmark.)

> Scope and verification

Inputs are complete finite rectangular rows with positive dimension. Widened
coordinate subtraction and hypot accumulation avoid the old squared-double
overflow/underflow; distances and sequential objective must still fit finite
double. Invalid shape is rejected before pair access. Trace admission counts one
record per input row, proposal and accepted state, not their inner values. The
separate complete n*n distance-cell ceiling is checked before allocation.
Defaults are one million records/cells, zero disables optional caps, and scalar
fitting has no trace quota. Neither ceiling bounds peak bytes; returned data,
states and proposals own their values.

Independent objectives, assignments and full local neighborhoods checked101
fits,226 accepted/initial states and4214 actual candidate records, including
all92 original points. Frozen checks preserved1000 integer-line fits exactly and
the original contaminated-blob medoid identities/assignments with objective
tolerance1e-10. Native controls cover duplicate identities, complete quotas,
ownership and finite-range failures. The ten-report shared build passed141
native CTest cases,45 directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour
sanitizer cases,90 compiled/executed public examples,412/412 scoped file
definitions plus11/11 changed lagged-Fibonacci engine definitions, and ten
representative clangd hovers. All four complete report programs executed and all
four native figures were visually reviewed. Receipts and repeatable checks are
retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-184-193/`.

> References

L. Kaufman and P. J. Rousseeuw(1990),
[Finding Groups in Data: An Introduction to Cluster Analysis|https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470316801].
The distinction between its BUILD/SWAP method and this random-start
exhaustive-swap variant is explicit above.
